<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="aligning-contexts" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Aligning the Three Contexts</title>

  <p>
    A culturally responsive after-school program will apply all six dimensions within each of the
    three contexts <mdash/> each dimension must be realized to carry a practice all the way to
    student growth. For example, we could design an activity to foster interpersonal relationships
    between students and mentors, but if mentors are not trained to properly run the activity it
    may not perform as expected. Therefore, not only do we need to apply each dimension at each
    level, but also make sure that the efforts at each level are in concert. A key good practice
    in this regard is to document the goals and expectations at each context, see how they
    interact, and assess if they are realistic, or which changes need to be made. Reflection
    sessions should be held on a regular basis, as a way to effectively change practices.
  </p>

  <p>
    One frequent error is making changes at each context but in different, disconnected or
    incompatible ways (sometimes even contradictory). You may still see some benefit in this
    case, but those changes are likely not as impactful as they could be, and may not be long
    term ones.
  </p>

</section>
